---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import t from '../i18n/en';
import { operator, repo } from '../lib/data';
---

<Base t={t} title="Privacy, Tunero" description="Privacy policy for tunero.dev." alternate="/de/datenschutz/">
  <section class="legal container">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p>
      This policy covers the website tunero.dev. What the Tunero app itself sends is in the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/PRIVACY.md`}>privacy policy of the app</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Controller</h2>
    <address>
      {operator.name}, {operator.street}, {operator.city}, {operator.country.en}<br />
      Email: <a href={`mailto:${operator.email}`}>{operator.email}</a>
    </address>

    <h2>In short</h2>
    <p>
      This website sets no cookies, uses no analytics and no ads, and loads no fonts, scripts or images from other servers.
      It is made of static files.
    </p>

    <h2>Hosting on GitHub Pages</h2>
    <p>
      The website is hosted on GitHub Pages, a service of GitHub, Inc., 88 Colin P. Kelly Jr. St., San Francisco, CA 94107,
      USA. When you open a page, GitHub processes your IP address to deliver it and, by its own account, stores it for
      security purposes. The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR: a legitimate interest in providing the website securely and
      reliably. GitHub participates in the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework and also uses the European Commission's standard
      contractual clauses. See the <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub Privacy Statement</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Domain and email at Cloudflare</h2>
    <p>
      The domain tunero.dev is managed through Cloudflare, Inc. Cloudflare answers the DNS queries for the domain but does
      not deliver the pages. When you write to {operator.email}, Cloudflare Email Routing forwards the message to my mailbox.
      I use what you send only to answer you (Art. 6(1)(b) and (f) GDPR) and delete the message once it is dealt with and no
      retention duty applies. See the <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare Privacy Policy</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Links to other sites</h2>
    <p>
      The download, the source code and the releases are on GitHub, and support goes through Ko-fi. When you follow one of
      these links, that provider's privacy policy applies.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You have the right of access, rectification, erasure and restriction of processing, the right to data portability, and
      the right to object to processing based on legitimate interest (Art. 15 to 21 GDPR). Write to {operator.email}. You can
      also complain to a data protection authority, for example the <a href="https://www.baden-wuerttemberg.datenschutz.de/">State Commissioner for Data Protection and Freedom of Information Baden-Württemberg</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
